 ],
 "literal": "High even if being, high those who are not, high they are not; low even if being, low those who are not, low they are not.",
 "translation": "Even if placed in a high position, those who lack greatness are not truly high. Even if placed in a low position, those who do not lack greatness are not truly low.",
 "modern": "உயர்ந்த நிலையில் இருந்தாலும், பெருமை இல்லாதவர்கள் உயர்ந்தவர்கள் ஆக மாட்டார்கள். தாழ்ந்த நிலையில் இருந்தாலும், சிறுமை இல்லாதவர்கள் தாழ்ந்தவர்கள் ஆக மாட்டார்கள்.",
 "explanation": "This couplet defines true greatness by separating it from mere social or material status. The words 'mēl' (high) and 'kīḻ' (low) are used in two senses: first to denote outward position, and second to denote inner character. Even if placed in a high position, people without noble qualities are not truly high. Conversely, people with noble qualities remain high in character even if they are placed in a low position.",
